Summary of important user-visible changes for drafting 0.1.0:
-------------------------------------------------------------------

 ** Initial release.  Thirty-one public functions across four namespaces plus
    the `draw.Drawing` class, 812 built-in self-tests and 67 `%!demo`
    blocks.  All geometry is in millimetres.

 ** `+geom` -- planar geometry, knowing nothing of file formats or drawings.
    Primitives: signed area, bounding box, centroid, affine transform,
    rectilinear test, rectilinear polygon offset, largest inscribed rectangle
    and triangulation.  Curve geometry: curvature, sampling, offsetting,
    self-intersection and arc length.  Construction geometry: line and circle
    intersections, tangent points from a point, and fillets, which return
    exactly the arguments `draw.Drawing.arc` takes.  Polylines can be
    resampled or simplified; `geom.simplify` is Douglas-Peucker and moves no
    point further than its tolerance, while `geom.resample` cuts corners by
    construction, so a polygon whose vertices matter wants the former.

 ** `+dxf` -- AutoCAD R12 (AC1009) ASCII DXF, read and write, covering LINE,
    POLYLINE, LWPOLYLINE, CIRCLE, ARC, TEXT, POINT and INSERT, with layers,
    line types, colours and a BLOCKS section.  R12 has no LWPOLYLINE, so an
    entity of that type is written as a POLYLINE with its geometry, layer and
    closed flag preserved.  `dxf.write` states `$LTSCALE` in the header, so a
    written file's dash lengths no longer depend on the recipient's setting.

 ** `+stl` -- binary STL from a *section stack*: a struct array of profile,
    z-range and holes, rather than a single extrusion, which expresses a
    stepped or eccentric shaft without leaving the planar model.  Each section
    is written as its own closed shell, so one section is a closed manifold
    and a stack of several is not -- slicers union it, a tool demanding a
    single closed surface will not.

 ** `+draw` -- `draw.Drawing`, a value class carrying lines, polylines with
    per-vertex bulges, arcs, circles, ellipses, text, hatches, blocks and
    inserts on named layers, with line types and colours.  Dimensions are
    stored semantically and carry no text of their own by default, so they
    stay truthful when the geometry changes: linear (`dim`), diameter, radius
    and angular, plus centre marks and leaders.  `transform` places a drawing
    and `merge` assembles several into a sheet; `draw.titleblock` frames it
    and `draw.coordtable` tabulates a profile that cannot be dimensioned.

 ** One lowering, three backends.  `entities` converts a drawing into a flat
    entity list, and `plot`, `tikz` and `dxf.write` all consume that list
    rather than walking the drawing themselves, so no backend can silently
    fail to know an entity type.  The three that take a drawing --- `plot`,
    `print` and `tikz` --- are methods, so they dispatch as `plot (D)` and
    leave the core functions of those names alone.  Line-type dash lengths
    follow one rule everywhere -- model units times a scale factor, as CAD's
    LTSCALE works -- and each backend defaults that factor to suit its medium.

 ** `draw.Drawing.plot` renders a drawing into a figure at equal aspect ratio,
    drawing all seven line types as themselves rather than mapping them onto
    the four styles a figure offers.  It keeps a margin between the geometry
    and the axes, and widens to hold text that would otherwise be cut at an
    edge.  It is a method rather than a namespace function, so `plot (D)` and
    `plot (hax, D)` both reach it while core `plot` is left alone.

 ** `draw.Drawing.print` plots a drawing onto paper at a stated scale, so a
    distance measured on the sheet times the scale denominator is the
    model.  The sheet is named (`A0` to `A5`, either orientation) or given in
    millimetres, and without a `Scale` the largest of the ISO 5455 preferred
    denominators that fits is chosen and returned.  Resolution is settable for
    a raster format; `.pdf`, `.eps` and `.svg` are vector and ignore it.  Pen
    width is a paper dimension and text is a model one, reduced by the scale,
    which is the convention `tikz` and CAD both follow.

 ** A DXF file reads back as a drawing, not as a heap of lines.
    `draw.fromentities` is the inverse of `entities`: it raises an entity list
    and its block definitions into a `draw.Drawing`, so a file can be
    transformed, merged, dimensioned further and written out again.  A
    dimension returns as a dimension and measures its geometry again rather
    than repeating the number it was written with.  What was lowered on the
    way out does not return: a hatch comes back as its boundary and fill
    lines, an ellipse as the polyline it was sampled to, because R12 records
    no memory of either having been anything else.  Whatever cannot be raised
    is reported through a second output rather than dropped.

 ** Dimensions are written as true `DIMENSION` entities, with the drawn
    picture in an anonymous block and a `DIMSTYLE` record naming the
    conventions, so a CAD application receives a dimension it can edit and
    re-measure instead of six loose lines stating a number.  All four families
    go: linear, angular, diameter and radius.  `entities` takes a
    `'dimensions'` pair to get the old exploded form back.

 ** `draw.Drawing.point` appends a single point, which is what a `POINT`
    entity in a file becomes on the way in.

 ** What R12 cannot carry is recorded, not hidden.  A hatch is generated as
    explicit fill lines, so nothing is lost and the recipient sees the section
    hatched; an ellipse is sampled to a closed polyline, and that *is* reported
    as a loss by the second output of `entities`.
